1.23.2: wipe and loupe follow the view, in every format and pairing

The wipe and the loupe were written around the current render; this
makes them about whatever is on view.

Every image on the page - the original, the look pinned as A, the
current render - is the input seen through a geometry (the original's
is the identity), brought to one input scale so a preview and a
full-size render compare. The wipe lays any pair of them by one
mapping: the other image's picture area onto the shown image's, clipped
to both and to the divider. The dropdown offers original, A and
current; when the choice is what is on view already, the natural
counterpart is used (current when viewing the original, the original
when viewing the current). So the original view wipes against a
Polaroid full-frame render on the picture, and A (a Polaroid) wipes
against a Super 8 current inside the border.

The loupe shows what is on view at full size: the current look
rendered full size, A's recipe rendered full size (render() now takes
a recipe object as the look), or the original itself - the file for a
JPEG, the straight print at full size for a raw. Each is made once and
kept until the look or the image changes.

Also: the native export and batch had been sending the look without
the Format choice (the page had two lookFromControls, and the older
one predated crop), so "Export next to source" and "Apply to folder"
ignored full frame. One function now, with crop.

Tests: the Chromium test checks the wipe's placement for six formats
(no crop, square, gate, instant crop, instant full, pack-film full)
in all three views against all three comparison choices - 54 cases,
the input's center pixel landing on the same screen point through
both images within 0.75 px - and the loupe's caption and size over the
original and over A.

